 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Bundle rules are recorded when you declare them and replayed onto the app at registration time.\n\n**Recording (declaration time)**\n- `Bundle.attach_route_rule` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:413`) doesn't touch the app. It validates that the endpoint and view name contain no dots, then calls `self.record(lambda s: s.attach_route_rule(...))`.\n- `Bundle.record` (`bundles.py:224`) appends that lambda to `self.deferred_functions`.\n- The route decorators on `Scaffold` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py`) end up in this `attach_route_rule`.\n\n**Replaying (registration time)**\n1. `App.register_bundle` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:571`) calls `bundle.register(app, options)`.\n2. `Bundle.register` (`bundles.py:273`) creates a `BundleSetupState` via `make_setup_state` (`bundles.py:246`).\n   - It also merges the bundle's handlers into the app with `_merge_bundle_funcs` (`bundles.py:379`).\n   - Then it loops `for deferred in self.deferred_functions: deferred(state)` (`bundles.py:334`).\n3. Each deferred lambda calls `BundleSetupState.attach_route_rule` (`bundles.py:87`), which:\n   - applies the `url_prefix`, `subdomain` and `url_defaults`;\n   - prefixes the endpoint with the bundle name (`{name_prefix}.{name}.{endpoint}`);\n   - calls `self.app.attach_route_rule(...)` (`sansio/app.py:606`), which adds the rule to the app's URL map.\n\n`src/tessel/bundles.py` only defines the thin `Bundle(SansioBundle)` subclass. The mechanism lives in `sansio/bundles.py`.",
